  <skos:Concept rdf:about="022f3225-3b23-4e56-a6ea-c21f08f9e179">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="f5717312-c3ca-4492-a166-9f17c6d9b273"/>
    <dcterms:created>2021-02-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SULFUR DIOXIDE SURFACE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-03-03 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Acidic gas, formula SO2, formed in the combustion of many fuels and in the oxidation of naturally occurring sulfur gases measured at the earth&apos;s surface. It is the primary sulfur gas emitted from combustion sources and is a precursor to sulfuric acid, which is a major constituent of acid rain.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=SULFUR DIOXIDE SURFACE [022f3225-3b23-4e56-a6ea-c21f08f9e179,560882] - SULFUR DIOXIDE [f5717312-c3ca-4492-a166-9f17c6d9b273,86733]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Sulfur_dioxide" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Acidic gas, formula SO2, formed in the combustion of many fuels and in the oxidation of naturally occurring sulfur gases measured at the earth&apos;s surface. It is the primary sulfur gas emitted from combustion sources and is a precursor to sulfuric acid, which is a major constituent of acid rain.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2021-03-03 09:13:04.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="01a4a324-cad3-441d-b0f1-02dc9742784a">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="dba19648-3f52-48ba-b00b-8527d44c4d74"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ELECTRICAL PROPERTIES</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="GISP2 (Greenland) Ice Cores and Climate Change: http://www.gisp2.sr.unh.edu/GISP2/MoreInfo/Ice_Cores_Past.html" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An important baseline measurement in ice cores is electrical conductivity.
Electrical conductivity measurements (ECM) of the core is a very rapid methodto indicate how acidic the core is without the chemical detail of the ionanalyses. The value of the measurement is that it can be done for the wholelength of the core in high resolution and provide an immediate picture of thecore and allow quick detection of interesting areas, such as a volcaniceruptions. Because it is a high resolution, continuous measurement it can beused, along with the other measurements, for time frequency analysis in orderto identify cycles in the climate signal.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
