  <skos:Concept rdf:about="05bef198-cfff-48be-b0cb-14e296d38dbc">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="3e822484-c94a-457b-a32f-376fcbd6fd35"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">TOPOGRAPHIC EFFECTS</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-16 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=http://www.forestry.umt.edu/
New Value=University of Montana, College of Forestry and Conservation</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-16 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=The topographic effect is indicated on Landsat images of  rugged terrain by
the visual impression of relief. It is caused by the  variation in
spectral radiance due to surface slope and aspect variations.  The
difference in radiance between a horizontal and sloping surface of the
same cover type provides a measure of the topographic effect (Holben and
Justice 1981).	Holben and Justice (1979) also measured it and showed that
the effect is most extreme in areas of rugged terrain and especially for
slopes in the principal plane of the sun and at low solar
elevations.
New Value=The topographic effect is indicated on Landsat images of rugged terrain by the visual impression of relief. It is caused by the  variation in spectral radiance due to surface slope and aspect variations. The difference in radiance between a horizontal and sloping surface of the same cover type provides a measure of the topographic effect (Holben and Justice 1981).	Holben and Justice (1979) also measured it and showed that
the effect is most extreme in areas of rugged terrain and especially for slopes in the principal plane of the sun and at low solar elevations.</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="University of Montana, College of Forestry and Conservation" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The topographic effect is indicated on Landsat images of rugged terrain by the visual impression of relief. It is caused by the  variation in spectral radiance due to surface slope and aspect variations. The difference in radiance between a horizontal and sloping surface of the same cover type provides a measure of the topographic effect (Holben and Justice 1981).	Holben and Justice (1979) also measured it and showed thatthe effect is most extreme in areas of rugged terrain and especially for slopes in the principal plane of the sun and at low solar elevations.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-08-16 09:54:58.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
