    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">MESOSPHERIC CLOUDS (OBSERVED/ANALYZED)</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-02-12 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=MESOSPHERIC CLOUDS (OBSERVED/ANALYZED) [04bc6942-12e0-413f-94d2-1ba7f5edf595,82119] - POLAR MESOSPHERIC CLOUDS [0a7f50ce-4968-46c8-86a6-23ea13c1830c,82139]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-10-09 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Also known as noctilucent or “night shining” clouds, form between 76 to 85 kilometers (47 to 53 miles) above the Earth’s surface, near the boundary of the mesosphere and thermosphere, a region known as the mesopause. At these altitudes, water vapor can freeze into clouds of ice crystals. When the Sun is below the horizon and the ground is in darkness, these high clouds may still be illuminated, lending them their ethereal, “night shining” qualities.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-02-12 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
