    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A phenomena wherein sea level rises above the normal tide level whenhurricanes or tropical storms move from the ocean along or across acoastal region. This sea level rise can consists of three components, thefirst of which results from low barometric pressure, i.e. the so-calledinverse barometer effect, where lower atmospheric pressure on the surfaceof the water allows it to rise. The second component is wind set-up wherethe winds drag surface</skos:definition>
