  <skos:Concept rdf:about="09a1b23e-bd8b-4bb9-966b-2388328973d4">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="94668478-3b79-4819-847e-b154bf241aa3"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-02-14</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">FRONTAL FOG</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-21 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Fog associated with frontal zones and frontal passages.

It is usually divided into three types: warm-front prefrontal fog; cold-front post-frontal fog; and frontal-passage fog. The first two types are a result of rain falling into cold stable air and raising the dewpoint temperature. Frontal-passage fog can result from the &quot;mixing of warm and cold air masses in the frontal zone&quot; or by &quot;sudden cooling of air over moist ground.&quot;
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-02-14 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=FRONTAL FOG [09a1b23e-bd8b-4bb9-966b-2388328973d4,82277] - FOG [94668478-3b79-4819-847e-b154bf241aa3,82267]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="American Meteorological Society (http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Main_Page)" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Fog associated with frontal zones and frontal passages.It is usually divided into three types: warm-front prefrontal fog; cold-front post-frontal fog; and frontal-passage fog. The first two types are a result of rain falling into cold stable air and raising the dewpoint temperature. Frontal-passage fog can result from the &apos;mixing of warm and cold air masses in the frontal zone&apos; or by &apos;sudden cooling of air over moist ground.&apos;</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-03-21 14:12:47.0</dcterms:modified>
