  <skos:Concept rdf:about="0bfb8ae4-c08a-4d69-82d2-1b1b0d4acef6">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="c7b5c02c-724d-4a19-b824-98180f3900c9"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">INDIGENOUS VEGETATION</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Allaby, M. 1998. A Dictionary of Ecology. Oxford University Press.  Oxford, New York." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Indigenous Vegetation or native vegetation is applied to a species thatoccurs naturally in an area, and therefore, one that has not beenintroduced by humans accidentally or intentionally.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="0a454dc9-de56-4682-8688-36ffd547d42f">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="d3594523-ba0d-4275-b121-95039f905058"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">HAPTOPHYTES</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2017-03-15 User Id=mgenazzi Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Definition added
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Haptophytes are a clade of heterokont algae with smooth flagella. Haptophytes are typically eukaryotic, with a central nucleus and are characterized with a haptonema organelle.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="lifeofplant.com" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="ab7eb13f-5fcb-4afa-8819-c37d36feeec1"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Haptophytes are a clade of heterokont algae with smooth flagella. Haptophytes are typically eukaryotic, with a central nucleus and are characterized with a haptonema organelle.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2017-03-15 11:15:38.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="174fa36e-3a06-40a1-bc95-87e6799bdead">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="0c279e58-9ad3-4748-816a-de8cabeaf0c4"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-02-18</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">PARABOLIC DUNE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-29 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: U-shaped mounds of sand with convex noses trailed by elongated arms are parabolic dunes. Sometimes these dunes are called U-shaped, blowout, or hairpin dunes, and they are well known in coastal deserts. Unlike crescent shaped dunes, their crests point upwind. The elongated arms of parabolic dunes follow rather than lead because they have been fixed by vegetation, while the bulk of the sand in the dune migrates forward.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-02-18 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=PARABOLIC DUNE [174fa36e-3a06-40a1-bc95-87e6799bdead,82591] - DUNES [0c279e58-9ad3-4748-816a-de8cabeaf0c4,82581]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="PhysicalGeography.net" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">U-shaped mounds of sand with convex noses trailed by elongated arms are parabolic dunes. Sometimes these dunes are called U-shaped, blowout, or hairpin dunes, and they are well known in coastal deserts. Unlike crescent shaped dunes, their crests point upwind. The elongated arms of parabolic dunes follow rather than lead because they have been fixed by vegetation, while the bulk of the sand in the dune migrates forward.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-03-29 10:20:04.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
