    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-19 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Pollen are several-celled microgametophyte of seed plants enclosed in a microspore wall. Fossil pollen consists entirely of the microspore wall. The study of pollen is called palynology or pollen analysis and is an important
aspect in reconstructing past climates. Paleoclimatic reconstructions by pollen analysis is possible because (1) pollen grains possess morphological characteristics that are specif to a particular genus or species of plant; (2)
they are produced in vast quantities by wind-pollinated plants and are distributed widely from their source; (3) they are extremely resistant to decay; and (4) they reflect the natural vegetation at the time of pollen
deposition, which can yield information about ast climatic conditions.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Jackson, J.A. 1997. Glossary of Geology, 4th edition. American Geological Institute Bradley, R.S. 1999. Paleoclimatology: Recnstructing Climates of the Quaternary, 2nd ed. Harcourt Academic Press." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Pollen are several-celled microgametophyte of seed plants enclosed in a microspore wall. Fossil pollen consists entirely of the microspore wall. The study of pollen is called palynology or pollen analysis and is an importantaspect in reconstructing past climates. Paleoclimatic reconstructions by pollen analysis is possible because (1) pollen grains possess morphological characteristics that are specif to a particular genus or species of plant; (2)they are produced in vast quantities by wind-pollinated plants and are distributed widely from their source; (3) they are extremely resistant to decay; and (4) they reflect the natural vegetation at the time of pollendeposition, which can yield information about ast climatic conditions.</skos:definition>
