    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-10-21 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: General elevation pattern of the sea ice surface. Changes in ice topography can be caused by water currents and wind, which can either pull ice apart or push it together to cause overriding. Some specific ice formations include rafting, ridged, and hummocked ice.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-10-21 User Id=tstevens Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=SEA ICE TOPOGRAPHY [15547b03-1b99-4c60-92eb-216a2908f504,778232] - DYNAMIC TOPOGRAPHY [940550d2-1d9f-4c28-b9ba-857c2dc8ef95,778236]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Farrell, S.L., K. Duncan, E.M. Buckley, J. Richter-Menge, R. Li, 2020, Mapping Sea Ice Surface Topography in High Fidelity with ICESat-2, Geophysical Research Letters, Vol 47, Issue 21. DOI 10.1029/2020GL090708" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">General elevation pattern of the sea ice surface. Changes in ice topography can be caused by water currents and wind, which can either pull ice apart or push it together to cause overriding. Some specific ice formations include rafting, ridged, and hummocked ice.</skos:definition>
