    <dcterms:created>2021-02-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">NOBLE GAS</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=NOBLE GAS [18d9ddbb-66cc-4b92-aa8d-2395ab3a17ce,560876] - ATMOSPHERIC CHEMISTRY [b9c56939-c624-467d-b196-e56a5b660334,83481]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=NOBLE GAS [18d9ddbb-66cc-4b92-aa8d-2395ab3a17ce,560876] - ATMOSPHERIC RADON [a81f6b17-2a16-4600-84b6-bcbcc5c29d2f,560877]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-03-04 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Noble gases makes up a class of chemical elements with similar properties; under standard conditions, they are all odorless, colorless, monatomic gases with very low chemical reactivity. The six naturally occurring noble gases are helium (He), neon (Ne), argon (Ar), krypton (Kr), xenon (Xe), and the radioactive radon (Rn). Oganesson (Og) is variously predicted to be a noble gas as well or to break the trend due to relativistic effects; its chemistry has not yet been investigated.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Renouf, Edward (1901). &apos;Noble gases&apos;. Science. 13 (320): 268–270. Bibcode:1901Sci....13..268R." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="a81f6b17-2a16-4600-84b6-bcbcc5c29d2f"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Noble gases makes up a class of chemical elements with similar properties; under standard conditions, they are all odorless, colorless, monatomic gases with very low chemical reactivity. The six naturally occurring noble gases are helium (He), neon (Ne), argon (Ar), krypton (Kr), xenon (Xe), and the radioactive radon (Rn). Oganesson (Og) is variously predicted to be a noble gas as well or to break the trend due to relativistic effects; its chemistry has not yet been investigated.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2021-03-04 08:25:32.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
