  <skos:Concept rdf:about="14fa5360-320c-4d54-9bf6-9871a4b308d7">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="3546cb0a-27a2-4914-85cf-1774b5c4ed19"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">MUSHROOMS</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Starr, C. and R. Taggart. 1998. Biology: the unity and diversity of life. Wadsworth Publishing Company. Belmont, California. and Gould, J. and W.Keaton. 1998. Biological Science.  W.W. Norton and Company. New York, New York." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Mushrooms are a type of fungi (which are major decomposers that engagein extracellular digestion and absorption of organic matter). Mushroomsare of the order Basidiomycota, the club fungi, which have a fruiting bodyin the aboveground portion of the plant.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="14555831-70ae-4650-8983-956d65595575">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="d81b77be-0177-4e26-942c-aa911239482d"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">WATER MANAGEMENT</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-11 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Water management is a very broad term which can include human direct
or indirect impacts on the water cycle.  Water management can involve (1)
changing the Earth&apos;s surface such as when dams are created to control
flooding (perhaps due to more deforested and overgrazed slopes), (2)
pollution control, when decisions are made to minimize the chemicals, such
as fertilizers, pesticides or road salt, that are used on the soil surface
in order to minimize the amount of pollutants that will leach into the
soil, and (3) amount and quality of water available for human use as when
water is used for irrigation, industry or for direct human
consumption.
New Value=Water management is a very broad term which can include human direct or indirect impacts on the water cycle.  Water management can involve (1) changing the Earth&apos;s surface such as when dams are created to control flooding (perhaps due to more deforested and overgrazed slopes), (2) pollution control, when decisions are made to minimize the chemicals, such as fertilizers, pesticides, or road salt, that are used on the soil surface in order to minimize the amount of pollutants that will leach into the soil, and (3) amount and quality of water available for human use as when water is used for irrigation, industry or for direct human consumption.</skos:changeNote>
