    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SALT FLUX</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2022-08-11 User Id=sritz Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/atmosphere/aerosols/sea-salt
New Value=https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/atmosphere/aerosols/sea-salt, http://www-das.uwyo.edu/~geerts/cwx/notes/chap11/ocean_transport.html, https://science.nasa.gov/earth-science/oceanography/physical-ocean/salinity</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2022-08-02 User Id=sritz Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=SALT FLUX [376dde0f-a750-4138-91ce-8ca635bf05bd,1011360] - OCEAN SALINITY [1a4e5774-7d4a-4ce7-9a4c-e2c72c8c377f,1011299]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2022-08-02 User Id=sritz Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Sea salt aerosol, which originally comes from sea spray, is one of the most widely distributed natural aerosols. Sea salt aerosols are characterized as non-light-absorbing, highly hygroscopic, and having coarse particle size. Some sea salt dominated aerosols could have a single scattering albedo as large as ~0.97. Due to the hygroscopy, a sea salt particle can serve as a very efficient cloud condensation nuclei (CCN), altering cloud reflectivity, lifetime, and precipitation process. According to the IPCC report, the total sea salt flux from ocean to atmosphere is ~3300 Tg/yr
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/atmosphere/aerosols/sea-salt, http://www-das.uwyo.edu/~geerts/cwx/notes/chap11/ocean_transport.html, https://science.nasa.gov/earth-science/oceanography/physical-ocean/salinity" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Sea salt aerosol, which originally comes from sea spray, is one of the most widely distributed natural aerosols. Sea salt aerosols are characterized as non-light-absorbing, highly hygroscopic, and having coarse particle size. Some sea salt dominated aerosols could have a single scattering albedo as large as ~0.97. Due to the hygroscopy, a sea salt particle can serve as a very efficient cloud condensation nuclei (CCN), altering cloud reflectivity, lifetime, and precipitation process. According to the IPCC report, the total sea salt flux from ocean to atmosphere is ~3300 Tg/yr</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2022-08-11 11:55:47.0</dcterms:modified>
