    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">LENGTH OF THE SOLAR CYCLE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-27 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=LENGTH OF THE SOLAR CYCLE [22c14e35-48a4-40b5-a503-add48c2d4cd4,40191] - SUNSPOT ACTIVITY [3429bc72-0780-44c8-9743-92f84118279d,40189]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-10-09 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The solar cycle is a nearly periodic change in the Sun&apos;s activity between the time where we can observe the most and least number of sunspots, and generally lasts around 11 years. Sometimes the surface of the Sun is very active with lots of sunspots, while other times it is quieter with only a few or even none.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/news/whats-the-big-deal-about-solar-cycles" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The solar cycle is a nearly periodic change in the Sun&apos;s activity between the time where we can observe the most and least number of sunspots, and generally lasts around 11 years. Sometimes the surface of the Sun is very active with lots of sunspots, while other times it is quieter with only a few or even none.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2024-10-09 09:19:14.0</dcterms:modified>
