  <skos:Concept rdf:about="29d6cd82-4762-4316-9fdd-29430dae7ad9">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="e1dff4d5-2e5b-46e7-9804-9de29fdb36d9"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-02-14</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">CUMULUS CASTELLANUS</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-02-14 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=CUMULUS CASTELLANUS [29d6cd82-4762-4316-9fdd-29430dae7ad9,82227] - CUMULUS [e1dff4d5-2e5b-46e7-9804-9de29fdb36d9,82215]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-21 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: (Previously called castellatus.) A cloud species of which at least a fraction of its upper part presents some vertically developed cumuliform protuberances (some of which are taller than they are wide) that give the cloud a crenellated or turreted appearance.

This castellanus character is especially evident when the cloud is seen from the side. The cumuliform cloud elements generally have a common base and usually seem to be arranged in lines. The species is found only in the genera cirrus, cirrocumulus, altocumulus, and stratocumulus. Cirrus castellanus differs from cirrocumulus castellanus in that its vertical protuberances subtend an angle of more than 1° when observed at an angle of more than 30° above the horizon. When altocumulus castellanus and stratocumulus castellanus attain a considerable vertical development, they become cumulus congestus and often develop into cumulonimbus. Stratocumulus castellanus should not be confused with stratocumulus pierced by cumulus.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="American Meteorological Society (http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Main_Page)" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">(Previously called castellatus.) A cloud species of which at least a fraction of its upper part presents some vertically developed cumuliform protuberances (some of which are taller than they are wide) that give the cloud a crenellated or turreted appearance.This castellanus character is especially evident when the cloud is seen from the side. The cumuliform cloud elements generally have a common base and usually seem to be arranged in lines. The species is found only in the genera cirrus, cirrocumulus, altocumulus, and stratocumulus. Cirrus castellanus differs from cirrocumulus castellanus in that its vertical protuberances subtend an angle of more than 1° when observed at an angle of more than 30° above the horizon. When altocumulus castellanus and stratocumulus castellanus attain a considerable vertical development, they become cumulus congestus and often develop into cumulonimbus. Stratocumulus castellanus should not be confused with stratocumulus pierced by cumulus.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-03-21 13:30:29.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="289ca013-0526-49e0-8b87-51513702e8f4">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="25d73bcf-c8d4-4c0e-ac98-8f3e98677e73"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-09-04</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">HEAT INDEX</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-10-11 User Id=saritz Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
User Note=added new definition
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The heat index (HI) or “apparent temperature” is an approximation of how hot it “feels” for a given combination of air temperature and relative humidity (RH). Generally, higher RH values at the same temperature feel warmer or more stressful because of less evaporative cooling when people perspire. The HI is the result of extensive biometeorological studies over a period of decades by various researchers, most notably Robert G. Steadman.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-09-04 User Id=epneff Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=HEAT INDEX [289ca013-0526-49e0-8b87-51513702e8f4,105823] - ATMOSPHERIC TEMPERATURE INDICES [25d73bcf-c8d4-4c0e-ac98-8f3e98677e73,105815]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Glossary of Meteorology, American Meteorological Society, 2013, http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Heat_index  Steadman, R. G., 1979: The assessment of sultriness. Part I: A temperature–humidity index based on human physiology and clothing science. J. Appl. Meteor., 18, 861–873." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The heat index (HI) or “apparent temperature” is an approximation of how hot it “feels” for a given combination of air temperature and relative humidity (RH). Generally, higher RH values at the same temperature feel warmer or more stressful because of less evaporative cooling when people perspire. The HI is the result of extensive biometeorological studies over a period of decades by various researchers, most notably Robert G. Steadman.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-10-11 18:04:36.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
