    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="691cb42a-9de2-4f49-b1b4-9a4be80abd2b"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">&apos;Glacial landforms&apos; are landforms derived from the erosion and depositioncaused by glaciers and ice sheets, associated meltwater, and the Earth&apos;srheological response.  Such landforms include drumlins, moraines, cirques,fjords, etc.  &apos;Glacial processes&apos; include deposition of sediments and erosionof the Earth&apos;s surface by grinding, scouring, and polishing effected by themovement of glacier ice armed with rock fragments frozen into it, together withthe erosive action of meltwater streams.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2024-10-03 13:50:02.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
    <skos:related rdf:resource="b895f4b5-5273-49ef-883f-b67d9f199505"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="3dfa8dcf-0df2-4654-ae3e-c97586265c3e">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="2bedb6b3-6e92-42e2-b382-60e2a6aab8e9"/>
    <dcterms:created>2012-06-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">GLACIATION</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-23 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: A glaciation (a created composite term meaning Glacial Period, referring to the Period or Era of, as well as the process of High Glacial Activity), often called an ice age, is a geological phenomenon in which massive ice sheets form in the Arctic and Antarctic and advance toward the equator. Conversely, the term interglacial or Interglacial Period, such as the current era, is used to denote the absence of large-scale glaciation on a global scale ¿ i.e., a non-Ice Age. Interglacials are, in general, shorter than glacial epochs.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-26 User Id=gee-cee Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=GLACIATION [3dfa8dcf-0df2-4654-ae3e-c97586265c3e,39985] - LAND RECORDS [2bedb6b3-6e92-42e2-b382-60e2a6aab8e9,39979]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaciation" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A glaciation (a created composite term meaning Glacial Period, referring to the Period or Era of, as well as the process of High Glacial Activity), often called an ice age, is a geological phenomenon in which massive ice sheets form in the Arctic and Antarctic and advance toward the equator. Conversely, the term interglacial or Interglacial Period, such as the current era, is used to denote the absence of large-scale glaciation on a global scale ¿ i.e., a non-Ice Age. Interglacials are, in general, shorter than glacial epochs.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-07-23 17:14:56.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="4fe601b0-314f-4f63-8ec1-3b96cc7263b8">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="2bedb6b3-6e92-42e2-b382-60e2a6aab8e9"/>
    <dcterms:created>2012-06-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">PALEOSOLS</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-23 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Paleosols are soils that formed in a landscape of the past with distinctive morphological features resulting from a soil-forming environment that no longer exists at the site.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-26 User Id=gee-cee Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=PALEOSOLS [4fe601b0-314f-4f63-8ec1-3b96cc7263b8,40009] - LAND RECORDS [2bedb6b3-6e92-42e2-b382-60e2a6aab8e9,39979]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Jackson, J.A., ed. 1997. Glossary of Geology, 4th edition. American Geological  Institute" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Paleosols are soils that formed in a landscape of the past with distinctive morphological features resulting from a soil-forming environment that no longer exists at the site.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-07-23 17:25:00.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="37ae8d4e-fe97-43d3-b8ee-a597e4ebfe87">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="25d73bcf-c8d4-4c0e-ac98-8f3e98677e73"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-09-04</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">RESIDENTIAL ENERGY DEMAND TEMPERATURE INDEX</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-09-04 User Id=epneff Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=RESIDENTIAL ENERGY DEMAND TEMPERATURE INDEX [37ae8d4e-fe97-43d3-b8ee-a597e4ebfe87,105827] - ATMOSPHERIC TEMPERATURE INDICES [25d73bcf-c8d4-4c0e-ac98-8f3e98677e73,105815]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-10-11 User Id=saritz Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
User Note=added new definition
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: NOAA’s Residential Energy Demand Temperature Index (REDTI) correlates heating and cooling degree-day data
with population. A hot summer in sparsely populated Wyoming will not have the same impact on energy use as a
hot summer in the Mid-Atlantic. June 2010 had the second highest REDTI on record due to especially hot
temperatures in the heavily populated South and Southeast regions. The REDTI is measured on a scale of zero to 100, with 100 being the highest population weighted degree day average and zero being the lowest.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Earth Gauge, http://www.earthgauge.net/wp-content/CF_Weather_and_Energy.pdf" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">NOAA’s Residential Energy Demand Temperature Index (REDTI) correlates heating and cooling degree-day datawith population. A hot summer in sparsely populated Wyoming will not have the same impact on energy use as ahot summer in the Mid-Atlantic. June 2010 had the second highest REDTI on record due to especially hottemperatures in the heavily populated South and Southeast regions. The REDTI is measured on a scale of zero to 100, with 100 being the highest population weighted degree day average and zero being the lowest.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-10-11 18:15:12.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="746c49af-3e36-4f0a-b488-e024314d6cfa">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="25d73bcf-c8d4-4c0e-ac98-8f3e98677e73"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-09-04</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">THAWING INDEX</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-10-11 User Id=saritz Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
User Note=added new definition
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: As used by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, the number of Fahrenheit degree- days (above and below 32°F) between the lowest and highest points on the cumulative degree- days time curve for one thawing season.
The thawing index determined from air temperatures at 4.5 ft above the ground is commonly designated the air thawing index, while that determined from temperatures immediately below a surface is called the surface thawing index.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-09-04 User Id=epneff Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=THAWING INDEX [746c49af-3e36-4f0a-b488-e024314d6cfa,105831] - ATMOSPHERIC TEMPERATURE INDICES [25d73bcf-c8d4-4c0e-ac98-8f3e98677e73,105815]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Glossary of Meteorology, American Meteorological Society, 2013, http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Thawing_index" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">As used by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, the number of Fahrenheit degree- days (above and below 32°F) between the lowest and highest points on the cumulative degree- days time curve for one thawing season.The thawing index determined from air temperatures at 4.5 ft above the ground is commonly designated the air thawing index, while that determined from temperatures immediately below a surface is called the surface thawing index.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-10-11 19:05:18.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
