  <skos:Concept rdf:about="835c5ec2-50e3-4bef-b380-9f74b143dac6">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="3f4cfc81-7745-43d9-b313-f68cdf72359b"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SEWAGE DISPOSAL</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-29 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Sewage, also called raw sewage or wastewater, is the total mixture
collected from all drains in homes, such as from tub, sink and toilet
drains, and from buildings.  The sewer system brings this sewage together
in larger and larger pipes, just as twigs of a tree come together
eventually into the trunk.  The pollutants in sewage can be divided into
the following four categories:	(1) debris and grit, (2) particulate
organic matter, (3) colloidal and dissolved organic material, and (4)
dissolved inorganic material.
New Value=Involves the transport of sewage through cities and other inhabited areas to sewage treatment plants to protect public health and prevent disease. Sewage is treated to control water pollution before discharge to surface waters.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-29 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Nebel, Bernard J. and Richard T. Wright.  1998.  Environmental Science: the Way
the World Works.-6th ed. Prentice Hall, Inc. Upper Saddle River, New Jersey.
New Value=Sewerage and Sewage Disposal: A Textbook. New York: McGraw-Hill.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-27 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=PrefLabel Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update PrefLabel
Old Value=SEWAGE
New Value=SEWAGE DISPOSAL</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Sewerage and Sewage Disposal: A Textbook. New York: McGraw-Hill." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Involves the transport of sewage through cities and other inhabited areas to sewage treatment plants to protect public health and prevent disease. Sewage is treated to control water pollution before discharge to surface waters.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-06-29 14:55:25.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="48671d9e-a627-4034-baec-201bda5d166d">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="3f4cfc81-7745-43d9-b313-f68cdf72359b"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">NUCLEAR RADIATION EXPOSURE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-29 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Nebel, Bernard J. and Richard T. Wright. 1998. Environmental Science: the Way
the World Works.-6th ed. Prentice Hall, Inc. Upper Saddle River, New Jersey.
New Value=Nebel, Bernard J. and Richard T. Wright. 1998. Environmental Science: the Way the World Works.-6th ed. Prentice Hall, Inc. Upper Saddle River, New Jersey.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-27 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=PrefLabel Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update PrefLabel
Old Value=NUCLEAR RADIATION
New Value=NUCLEAR RADIATION EXPOSURE</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-29 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Nuclear radiation is a result of nuclear energy, such as, uranium,
undergoing fission. The products of such fission are atoms of lighter
elements and are generally unstable isotopes of their respective elements.
These unstable isotopes become stable by spontaneously ejecting subatomic
particles (alpha particles, beta particles, and neutrons), or high-energy
radiation (gamma rays), or both.
New Value=Nuclear radiation is a result of nuclear energy, such as uranium, undergoing fission.</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Nebel, Bernard J. and Richard T. Wright. 1998. Environmental Science: the Way the World Works.-6th ed. Prentice Hall, Inc. Upper Saddle River, New Jersey." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Nuclear radiation is a result of nuclear energy, such as uranium, undergoing fission.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-06-29 14:46:41.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="82eac236-38ba-469d-837a-950ffa7e8316">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="3ab3aa92-9cca-4660-a0ed-281fff07eede"/>
