    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ELECTRIC/MAGNETIC FIELD EXPOSURE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-27 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=ELECTRIC/MAGNETIC FIELD EXPOSURE [69d84440-b806-4093-a659-f052185e22bd,40211] - ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACTS [3f4cfc81-7745-43d9-b313-f68cdf72359b,20271]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-11 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The exposure of the magnetic field. An electric field is produced by voltage, which is the pressure used to push the electrons through the wire, much like water being pushed through a pipe. As the voltage increases, the electric field increases in strength. A magnetic field results from the flow of current through wires or electrical devices and increases in strength as the current increases. These two fields together are referred to as electric and magnetic fields, or EMFs.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="National Cancer Institute" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The exposure of the magnetic field. An electric field is produced by voltage, which is the pressure used to push the electrons through the wire, much like water being pushed through a pipe. As the voltage increases, the electric field increases in strength. A magnetic field results from the flow of current through wires or electrical devices and increases in strength as the current increases. These two fields together are referred to as electric and magnetic fields, or EMFs.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-07-11 09:57:28.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
