    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SKIN TEMPERATURE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-16 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=&quot;http://www-ocean.tamu.edu/~baum/paleo/paleogloss/paleogloss.html&quot;
New Value=Department of Oceanography, Texas A &amp; M University</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-23 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Skin temperature refers to the effective radiating temperature of the soil plus canopy surface. It is inferred from satellites in the 8-12 um window region. In climate models, it is the temperature used to determine upward thermal emission. The skin temperature usually shows a larger diurnal variation than the surface air temperature, a factor that needs to be considered when evaluating data/model comparisons.
New Value=Refers to the effective radiating temperature of the soil plus canopy surface. It is inferred from satellites in the 8-12 um window region. In climate models, it is the temperature used to determine upward thermal emission. The skin temperature usually shows a larger diurnal variation than the surface air temperature, a factor that needs to be considered when evaluating data/model comparisons.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-16 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Skin temperature refers to the effective radiating temperature of the soil
plus canopy surface. It is inferred from satellites in the 8-12 um window
region. In climate models, it is the temperature used to determine upward
thermal emission. The skin temperature usually shows a larger diurnal
variation than the surface air temperature, a factor that needs to be
considered when evaluating data/model comparisons. See [R. E.  Dickinson.
Land surface. In K. E. Trenberth, editor, Climate System Modeling, pages
149-171. Cambridge Univ. Press, 1992].
New Value=Skin temperature refers to the effective radiating temperature of the soil plus canopy surface. It is inferred from satellites in the 8-12 um window region. In climate models, it is the temperature used to determine upward thermal emission. The skin temperature usually shows a larger diurnal variation than the surface air temperature, a factor that needs to be considered when evaluating data/model comparisons.</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Department of Oceanography, Texas A &amp; M University" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Refers to the effective radiating temperature of the soil plus canopy surface. It is inferred from satellites in the 8-12 um window region. In climate models, it is the temperature used to determine upward thermal emission. The skin temperature usually shows a larger diurnal variation than the surface air temperature, a factor that needs to be considered when evaluating data/model comparisons.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-08-23 10:25:54.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
