  <skos:Concept rdf:about="5c58acfc-04ed-4cbf-8674-13c41b3e950d">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="492ffe26-8fbe-4d7d-a537-495fb96bdcce"/>
    <dcterms:created>2017-04-20</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">DIVERGENCE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2017-04-21 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Pressure fluctuations at the Earth&apos;s surface are produced by movements of air which produce changes in the mass of air in a vertical column above the ground. A net gain in mass produces an increase in pressure leading to a high pressure system. A net loss of mass gives rise to a low. When there is a horizontal flow of air into a region (convergence) a vertical upward movement of air occurs otherwise there would be a continual increase in density of the air. Divergence occurs when there is a net horizontal outward flow of air from a region. This leads to a vertical downward movement of air.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2017-04-20 User Id=sritz Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=DIVERGENCE [5c58acfc-04ed-4cbf-8674-13c41b3e950d,278863] - WIND DYNAMICS [492ffe26-8fbe-4d7d-a537-495fb96bdcce,278857]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="University of Sydney, School of Physics" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Pressure fluctuations at the Earth&apos;s surface are produced by movements of air which produce changes in the mass of air in a vertical column above the ground. A net gain in mass produces an increase in pressure leading to a high pressure system. A net loss of mass gives rise to a low. When there is a horizontal flow of air into a region (convergence) a vertical upward movement of air occurs otherwise there would be a continual increase in density of the air. Divergence occurs when there is a net horizontal outward flow of air from a region. This leads to a vertical downward movement of air.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2017-04-21 11:15:59.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="4a031bdf-a6c6-40b8-9c92-34cd83a5739e">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="4b982bef-56fe-41e9-a131-af575a8fec6a"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-08-05</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SEDIMENT TRANSPORT</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-05 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=SEDIMENT TRANSPORT [4a031bdf-a6c6-40b8-9c92-34cd83a5739e,105361] - FLUVIAL PROCESSES [4b982bef-56fe-41e9-a131-af575a8fec6a,84331]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-15 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The movement of solid particles (sediment), typically due to a combination of the force of gravity acting on the sediment, and/or the movement of the fluid in which the sediment is entrained. An understanding of sediment transport is typically used in natural systems, where the particles are clastic rocks (sand, gravel, boulders, etc.), mud, or clay; the fluid is air, water, or ice; and the force of gravity acts to move the particles due to the sloping surface on which they are resting.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="PhysicalGeography.net" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The movement of solid particles (sediment), typically due to a combination of the force of gravity acting on the sediment, and/or the movement of the fluid in which the sediment is entrained. An understanding of sediment transport is typically used in natural systems, where the particles are clastic rocks (sand, gravel, boulders, etc.), mud, or clay; the fluid is air, water, or ice; and the force of gravity acts to move the particles due to the sloping surface on which they are resting.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-08-15 13:57:45.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
