    <dcterms:created>2013-02-14</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">RADIATIONAL COOLING</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-21 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: In meteorology, the result of radiative cooling of the earth&apos;s surface and adjacent air.

Radiational cooling occurs, as is typical on calm, clear nights, whenever the longwave emission from the surface is not balanced by significant amounts of absorbed shortwave radiation or downwelling longwave from the atmosphere above the surface, and there are no nonradiative sources of sufficient energy to make up the difference.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-02-14 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=RADIATIONAL COOLING [5bac3ef6-5e30-4f14-a5dc-8065c7fcba55,82289] - CLOUD DYNAMICS [62019831-aaba-4d63-a5cd-73138ccfa5d0,82131]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="American Meteorological Society (http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Main_Page)" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">In meteorology, the result of radiative cooling of the earth&apos;s surface and adjacent air.Radiational cooling occurs, as is typical on calm, clear nights, whenever the longwave emission from the surface is not balanced by significant amounts of absorbed shortwave radiation or downwelling longwave from the atmosphere above the surface, and there are no nonradiative sources of sufficient energy to make up the difference.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-03-21 14:16:13.0</dcterms:modified>
