    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">TEMPERATURE-HUMIDITY INDEX</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-25 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=TEMPERATURE-HUMIDITY INDEX [5bdc74e2-ea3a-4d1d-b64e-9eaf3a879545,39683] - HUMIDITY INDICES [b881cf8f-7260-4980-80bc-4b6ae3716c39,23137]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2020-02-14 User Id=tstevens Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=DELETE 
User Note=Remove Concepts
System Note=delete narrower relation
Old Value=TEMPERATURE-HUMIDITY INDEX [5bdc74e2-ea3a-4d1d-b64e-9eaf3a879545,86841] - THI [9130e78a-882c-4a69-b1ae-0b775869c3de,86845]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-05 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: temperature–humidity index (THI), combination of temperature and humidity that is a measure of the degree of discomfort experienced by an individual in warm weather; it was originally called the discomfort index. The index is essentially an effective temperature based on air temperature and humidity; it equals 15 plus 0.4 times the sum of simultaneous readings of the dry- and wet-bulb temperatures. Thus, if the dry-bulb temperature is 90° F (32° C) and the wet-bulb temperature is 50° F (10° C), the discomfort index is 15 + 0.4 (140), or 71. Most people are quite comfortable when the index is below 70 and very uncomfortable when the index is above 80 to 85. In the U.S. the highest average daily values of the THI, exceeding 80, consistently occur in the southern California deserts and southwestern Arizona in July and August.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-25 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=TEMPERATURE-HUMIDITY INDEX [5bdc74e2-ea3a-4d1d-b64e-9eaf3a879545,39683] - THI [9130e78a-882c-4a69-b1ae-0b775869c3de,39687]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Encyclopædia Britannica Online, s. v. &apos;temperature-humidity index (THI)&apos;, accessed July 05, 2012, http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/586706/temperature-humidity-index-THI." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">temperature–humidity index (THI), combination of temperature and humidity that is a measure of the degree of discomfort experienced by an individual in warm weather; it was originally called the discomfort index. The index is essentially an effective temperature based on air temperature and humidity; it equals 15 plus 0.4 times the sum of simultaneous readings of the dry- and wet-bulb temperatures. Thus, if the dry-bulb temperature is 90° F (32° C) and the wet-bulb temperature is 50° F (10° C), the discomfort index is 15 + 0.4 (140), or 71. Most people are quite comfortable when the index is below 70 and very uncomfortable when the index is above 80 to 85. In the U.S. the highest average daily values of the THI, exceeding 80, consistently occur in the southern California deserts and southwestern Arizona in July and August.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2020-02-14 12:45:02.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="60bd0b0a-2d6f-4f3c-bf42-2c081ef48b72">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="58f39353-7e1c-4884-9501-376cd0377fbf"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SPECIES COMPETITION</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Krebs, Charles K. Ecology - 4th ed. 1994.  HarperCollins College Publishers, New York, New York." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Competition is the interaction of two species where they both use thesame limited resource or harm one another while seeking a resource.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="5beaf99c-0675-4af3-9236-f55d8d206d85">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="cac27b59-7810-4132-87b4-53108663584e"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SLEET</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2014-04-14 User Id=128.183.164.42 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=DELETE 
User Note=Move Concepts
