    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SNOW GRAINS</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2015-03-04 User Id=saritz Entity=AltLabel Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert AltLabel
New Value=id: null
text: Granular Snow
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2014-04-14 User Id=128.183.164.42 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
User Note=added definition
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=The Weather Channel Glossary
New Value=Glossary of Meteorology, American Meteorological Society, 2013,  http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Snow_grains</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2014-04-14 User Id=128.183.164.42 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
User Note=added definition
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Frozen precipitation in the form of very small, white, opaque grains of ice
New Value=(Also called granular snow.) Precipitation in the form of very small, white opaque particles of ice; the solid equivalent of drizzle.

They resemble snow pellets in external appearance, but are more flattened and elongated, and generally have diameters of less than 1 mm; they neither shatter nor bounce when they hit a hard surface. Descriptions of the physical structure of snow grains vary widely and include very fine, simple ice crystals; tiny, complex snow crystals; small, compact bundles of rime; and particles with a rime core and a fine glaze coating. It is agreed that snow grains usually fall in very small quantities, mostly from stratus clouds or from fog, and never in the form of a shower.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2014-04-14 User Id=128.183.164.42 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=SNOW GRAINS [6a16461a-49b9-4887-802f-2320c6dc4dd2,106345] - SNOW [b51b3708-a662-4cf1-bf13-e67f36b001c4,85501]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2014-04-14 User Id=128.183.164.42 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
User Note=added definition
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Frozen precipitation in the form of very small, white, opaque grains of ice
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Glossary of Meteorology, American Meteorological Society, 2013,  http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Snow_grains" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">(Also called granular snow.) Precipitation in the form of very small, white opaque particles of ice; the solid equivalent of drizzle.They resemble snow pellets in external appearance, but are more flattened and elongated, and generally have diameters of less than 1 mm; they neither shatter nor bounce when they hit a hard surface. Descriptions of the physical structure of snow grains vary widely and include very fine, simple ice crystals; tiny, complex snow crystals; small, compact bundles of rime; and particles with a rime core and a fine glaze coating. It is agreed that snow grains usually fall in very small quantities, mostly from stratus clouds or from fog, and never in the form of a shower.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2015-03-04 16:50:20.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
    <gcmd:altLabel gcmd:text="Granular Snow" xml:lang="en"/>
  </skos:Concept>
