New Value=MASS EXTINCTIONS [703d0c14-1978-4e7f-a51a-233c695823b9,40077] - PALEOCLIMATE INDICATORS [c9a5b3eb-7556-41a8-a2b8-c015db80e5b2,39947]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-24 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: mass extinction is a sharp decrease in the diversity and abundance of macroscopic life. They occur when the rate of extinction increases with respect to the rate of speciation. Because the majority of diversity and biomass on Earth is microbial, and thus difficult to measure, recorded extinction events affect the easily observed, biologically complex component of the biosphere rather than the total diversity and abundance of life.[1]
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extinction_event#cite_note-0  [1]  Nee, S. (2004). &apos;Extinction, slime, and bottoms&apos;. PLoS Biology 2 (8): E272. DOI:10.1371/journal.pbio.0020272. PMC 509315. PMID 15314670" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">mass extinction is a sharp decrease in the diversity and abundance of macroscopic life. They occur when the rate of extinction increases with respect to the rate of speciation. Because the majority of diversity and biomass on Earth is microbial, and thus difficult to measure, recorded extinction events affect the easily observed, biologically complex component of the biosphere rather than the total diversity and abundance of life.[1]</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-07-24 15:23:11.0</dcterms:modified>
