    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">POLYMERASE CHAIN REACTION</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-02-28 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Polymerase chain reaction (abbreviated PCR) is a laboratory technique for rapidly producing (amplifying) millions to billions of copies of a specific segment of DNA, which can then be studied in greater detail. PCR involves using short synthetic DNA fragments called primers to select a segment of the genome to be amplified, and then multiple rounds of DNA synthesis to amplify that segment.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-02-28 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=POLYMERASE CHAIN REACTION [7b989ab0-eaed-4698-87d8-f915c505b076,1603385] - OMICS METHODOLOGY [d2f152e1-7398-44ad-b0a4-77ee6ef5bd6c,1603367]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Polymerase-Chain-Reaction" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Polymerase chain reaction (abbreviated PCR) is a laboratory technique for rapidly producing (amplifying) millions to billions of copies of a specific segment of DNA, which can then be studied in greater detail. PCR involves using short synthetic DNA fragments called primers to select a segment of the genome to be amplified, and then multiple rounds of DNA synthesis to amplify that segment.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2024-02-28 14:56:24.0</dcterms:modified>
